  "query_preview": "/TUESDAY NIGHT THROUGH SUNDAY/...\nIssued at 342 PM MST Sun Nov 1 2026\n\nAs we start off the long term period, lets take a quick look at the \nsynoptic setup, we have a deep trough draped south across the \nCentral Plains, just to our east, while ridging builds in from the \nwest. This ridge will keep us in a dry northerly to northeasterly \nflow regime across our CWA with plentiful sunshine making a \ntriumphant return. As we progress into Wednesday, weak ridging is in \nplace overhead while model consensus has another cut-off low diving \nsoutheast from Idaho. This feature will bring an increase chance of \nprecipitation primarily across the Sierra Madre and Snowy Ranges. \nWhere at the higher elevations could get and inch or two of fresh \nsnow. Otherwise, for the remainder of the CWA, this system will not \nhave any impacts elsewhere as it continues to dive southeast across \nthe Four Corners region through Wednesday. Quickly behind it, \nridging nudges back in late Wednesday into Thursday. By the tail end \nof the week into next weekend, there are some differences in model \nsolutions with the placement and amplitude of synoptic features. \nWith the ECMWF having a deep trough just to our east while the GFS \nhas the trough slightly further east over the Great Lakes. However, \nmost model solutions have a ridge in place over our region with dry \nnorthwesterly flow. With all that being said, stay tuned to the \nforecast for future updates.\n\nNow, switching gears and taking a closer look at temperatures for \nthe long term period, expect highs to climb into the mid to upper \n40s, possibly a few location topping out in the lower 50s, west of I-\n25. While temperatures will soar into the lower to mid 50s east of I-\n25 for the remainder of the work week into the weekend. In general, \nexpect lows to dip into the lower to mid 30s across the CWA, \npossibly slightly colder west of the I-25 corridor.",
